---
description: |
  Writes the latest TruePattern Sigma rules into one multi-document YAML file
  for the `sigma` operator, regardless of whether license metadata is present.

  Run the operator on a complete walk of the feed: it replaces the file with
  the rules of its input, so a partial input, such as from an `added_after`
  poll, would remove all other rules. If no rule qualifies for the file, the
  operator writes an empty YAML stream so that `sigma` clears obsolete rules.
  A failed walk leaves the existing file unchanged.

  Every rule must be a single YAML document. The operator removes a leading
  document marker and a final document end marker, preserving comments and
  notices, including comments attached to the markers. It includes supplied
  STIX external references as a JSON-encoded YAML comment on the rule. It skips
  rules with several YAML documents, such as correlation rules, and warns
  about each one. It also skips rules whose YAML does not parse. If several
  rules share a Sigma rule ID, it selects the most recently modified one,
  regardless of whether that version has license metadata.

  The operator forwards the STIX Indicators of the rules that it wrote, so that
  you can continue with `truepattern::ocsf::normalize` and
  `truepattern::update_context`.

  With `product`, the file only holds the rules for that Sigma
  `logsource.product`, such as `windows`. The operator selects them after it
  resolves duplicate Sigma rule IDs, so the file is a subset of the complete
  file from the same walk, including when no rule qualifies for the product.

  With `logsource`, only rules whose complete logsource record equals the
  supplied record qualify. This excludes rules with additional constraints,
  including category, service, or definition. Use it to scope native events;
  `product` alone does not establish logsource compatibility.
args:
  named:
    - name: path
      type: string
      description: The rule file to write, such as `/var/lib/tenzir/truepattern/sigma.yaml`.
    - name: product
      type: string
      default: null
      description: |
        Only writes the rules with this Sigma `logsource.product`, such as
        `windows`. Defaults to all rules.
    - name: logsource
      default: null
      description: |
        Only writes rules with this exact Sigma logsource record. For example,
        `{product: "windows", service: "security"}` excludes category-scoped
        rules and rules for other services. Defaults to all logsources.
---

where type == "indicator"
// A format change must not resurrect an older Sigma version of the rule.
truepattern::latest
where pattern_type == "sigma"
// Normalize line endings and remove only document markers, not comments or
// notices before, after, or attached to them.
_text = pattern.replace("\r\n", "\n")
_text = _text.replace_regex(r"^(([ \t]*(#[^\n]*)?\n)*)---([ \t]*(#[^\n]*)?(\n|$))", r"\1\4")
// A final end marker may be followed by blank lines and comments.
_text = _text.replace_regex(r"(?m)^\.\.\.([ \t]*(#[^\n]*)?(\n[ \t]*(#[^\n]*)?)*)\z", r"\1")
// Any remaining document marker at the start of a line separates documents.
assert not _text.match_regex(r"(?m)^(---|\.\.\.)([ \t#]|$)"),
  message=f"skipping Sigma rule {id}: not a single YAML document"
_rule = _text.parse_yaml()
where _rule != null
// The latest version comes first, so `deduplicate` keeps it.
deduplicate _rule.id? else id
where $product == null or _rule.logsource?.product? == $product
where $logsource == null or _rule.logsource? == $logsource
drop _rule
// Carry all supplied source/license references and their descriptions into
// the executable file without changing the Sigma rule's own metadata.
if external_references? != null {
  _text = f"# TruePattern external references: {external_references.print_ndjson()}\n{_text}"
}
// A stable order keeps the documents in place across updates.
sort id
fork {
  summarize rules=collect(_text)
  // An empty list becomes a blank line: a valid YAML stream with no rules.
  select text=rules.join("\n---\n")
  // Write only after the walk completes. This is not an atomic replacement.
  to_file $path {
    write_lines
  }
}
drop _text
